\ cv8b-kfast.4 - kernel words made faster for the byte-header system
\ (Iteration 49: the owner asked for a look through the Forth sources).
\ Loaded after cv8b.4, only for designs with the gene kfast (lab/evolve);
\ each word here is an X8, so the converter gives the kernel's word its
\ body and every caller gets it.
\
\ NAME>BUF zero-filled all 32 bytes of NAMEBUF before every dictionary
\ lookup - FILL a byte at a time, about 380 dispatches a lookup: 25-27% of
\ the kernel, parse and corpus workloads' dispatches on a seed-10 front
\ design. The padding is for the cell kernel's SEARCH-WORDLIST, which
\ compares names a cell at a time; cv8b.4's SEARCH-WORDLIST8 compares the
\ count and that many bytes, and nothing else here reads NAMEBUF.

: NAME>BUF8 ( c-addr u --- )
\ Move the name c-addr u to NAMEBUF - no padding: nothing reads past it.
  32 MIN NAMEBUF PLACE ;

\ The thread walk (Iteration 51) - after NAME>BUF's fill, the next largest
\ cost: NEXT-NFA8, SEARCH-WORDLIST8's loop and NAME=? were 37-63% of the
\ kernel, parse and corpus workloads' dispatches, ~20 a candidate word.
\ Factored out of cv8b.4's SEARCH-WORDLIST8 unchanged, so that it can be
\ an opcode (THREAD-FIND in OPS10_POOL; the converter checks this body).

: THREAD-FIND ( nfa namebuf --- nfa' | 0 )
\ From nfa back along its thread: the first word whose count and name
\ are namebuf's, or 0.
  >R BEGIN DUP WHILE
    DUP C@ 31 AND R@ C@ = IF
      DUP 1+ R@ 1+ R@ C@ NAME=? IF R> DROP EXIT THEN
    THEN
    NEXT-NFA8
  REPEAT R> DROP ;

: SEARCH-WORDLIST8 ( c-addr u wid --- 0 | xt 1 | xt -1 )
\ cv8b.4's, its walk THREAD-FIND.
  ROT ROT NAME>BUF
  DUP >R NAMEBUF COUNT R@ @ HASH 1+ CELLS + @ R> DROP   ( nfa )
  NAMEBUF THREAD-FIND
  DUP IF DUP NAME>8 SWAP C@ 64 AND IF 1 ELSE -1 THEN THEN ;
